@Tutorial(time: 90) {
    @Intro(title: "Complete MDE Workflow: From Metamodel to Code") {
        Experience the full model-driven engineering workflow by building a complete chain from metamodel design to compiled, checked code.

        In this tutorial, you'll design an e-commerce metamodel with business rules, create and validate instance models, query them with AQL, transform them into a reporting model with ATL, generate Swift, JSON and Markdown with MTL, and verify the result end to end. Every step runs the real Swift Modelling tools on files you create in an empty directory.

        @Image(source: "complete-mde-workflow.svg", alt: "The complete MDE workflow: an Ecore metamodel with OCL invariants defines the shop data and test instances, which are validated and queried; an ATL transformation turns the shop data into a sales report; MTL templates generate a Swift API, JSON and Markdown from it; a Swift package and end-to-end checks verify the artefacts.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Designing the Metamodel") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Every MDE project starts with metamodel design. We'll create a small e-commerce metamodel that captures the essential concepts, their relationships and the business rules that valid data must follow.

            This metamodel is the foundation for every later step: the instances conform to it, the transformation reads it and the validation enforces its rules.

            @Image(source: "ecommerce-metamodel.svg", alt: "The shop metamodel: a Shop contains categories, products, customers and orders; a product belongs to a category, an order belongs to a customer and contains order items that refer to products. Products, customers and order items carry OCL invariants.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the core e-commerce metamodel.

                @Code(name: "ECommerce.ecore", file: workflow-01-step-01-metamodel.ecore)

                A `Shop` contains categories, products, customers and orders, and an order contains its items. `Product.category` and `Category.products` are opposite references, as are `Order.customer` and `Customer.orders`, so each relationship can be navigated from both ends while the data store only one end. Multiplicities state the simplest rules: every order has a payment method and at least one item (`items` is `1..*`), and the date of an order is an `EDate`. The OCL annotations state the other business rules as invariants: prices are positive, stock is never negative, email addresses contain `@` and every item orders at least one unit.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the metamodel structure.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-02-validate-metamodel.sh)

                `swift-ecore validate` checks that the metamodel is well-formed Ecore: names, bounds, opposite references and annotations. With `--verbose` it also lists what it found. This catches design issues before any instance depends on them.
            }

            @Step {
                Document the metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-03-metamodel-docs.sh)

                `swift-ecore inspect --detail full` describes every class with its attributes, references, multiplicities, opposites and invariants; `--show-references` and `--show-supertypes` add the reference graph and the inheritance. Written to a file with `-o`, this description can be shared with stakeholders and team members.
            }

            @Step {
                Export the metamodel structure as JSON.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-04-metamodel-export.sh)

                With `--format json`, `swift-ecore inspect` writes the same description as JSON, which web-based tools and scripts can read without an Ecore parser. Model instances, in contrast, are converted between XMI and JSON with `swift-ecore convert`.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Creating Model Instances") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With a validated metamodel, we can create instance models that represent actual business data. These instances serve as input for queries, transformations and code generation.

            Instances should reflect realistic scenarios, including edge cases such as a shop without orders, so that transformations and generators are tested on more than one shape of data.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a sample e-commerce instance.

                @Code(name: "shop-data.xmi", file: workflow-01-step-05-instance.xmi)

                The shop has four categories, ten products, five customers and eight orders. Objects that are referred to carry an `xmi:id`: each product names its category, each order its customer and each order item its product. The opposite ends, such as the products of a category or the orders of a customer, follow from these references when the model is loaded.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the instance against the metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-06-validate-instance.sh)

                Validation with `--metamodel` checks every object against its class: feature types, multiplicities and reference targets. It also evaluates every invariant for every object it applies to: two for each of the ten products and one for each customer and order item, 40 checks in all. `swift-ecore inspect` then counts the objects of each class.
            }

            @Step {
                Query the instance with AQL.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-07-query-instance.sh)

                `swift-aql query` evaluates AQL expressions on the model, with `self` bound to the shop or, with `--context`, to the object with that identifier or name. The queries navigate opposite references, select with `->select(p | ...)`, follow references from orders to products and aggregate with `->collect(...)->sum()`. The figures, such as a revenue of 6815.0 from 20 units in 8 orders, are the ones the report must reproduce later.
            }

            @Step {
                Write a script that creates additional test instances.

                @Code(name: "create-test-instances.sh", file: workflow-01-step-08-create-test-instances.sh)

                `swift-ecore create` creates a new model with one root object of a metamodel class. The other instances are small hand-written XMI files: a shop without orders, the smallest complete shop and a shop that deliberately breaks the business rules.
            }

            @Step {
                Create and validate the test instances.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-08-validate-test-instances.sh)

                Given several files, `swift-ecore validate` checks each of them in turn and ends with a count of valid and invalid files. The empty shop and the single-order shop are valid; the empty shop has no objects that an invariant applies to. The new shop from `swift-ecore create` lacks the name that the metamodel requires, and the invalid shop violates three invariants and has an order without items, each reported with the object and its location. `swift-ecore validate` then exits with status 1, so a script can stop at an invalid model.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Model Transformation with ATL") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ATL transformations reshape model data for different purposes. We'll transform the operational e-commerce model into a reporting model that holds sales figures instead of individual orders.

            This transformation demonstrates how ATL adapts model structures to different requirements, and how the result can be checked against the source.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Design the target reporting metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Reporting.ecore", file: workflow-01-step-09-reporting-metamodel.ecore)

                A `SalesReport` holds the key figures of a shop and one metric for each category, customer and product. Its invariants require the category sales to add up to the total revenue and the orders of the customers to add up to the total number of orders.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the ATL transformation.

                @Code(name: "ECommerce2Reporting.atl", file: workflow-01-step-10-transformation.atl)

                Helpers compute the value of an order item, the total of an order and the units and revenue of a product. Four matched rules turn the shop into a report and each category, customer and product into its metric. Assigning `s.categories` to `categoryMetrics` uses the metrics that the rules created for those categories.
            }

            @Step {
                Execute the transformation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-11-run-transformation.sh)

                `--validate-only` checks the rules and helpers against both metamodels without running them. `--source-metamodel` and `--target-metamodel` bind the metamodels `Shop` and `Reporting` of the module header to their files. The result is a report with 20 objects.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the transformation results.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-12-validate-transformation.sh)

                The report satisfies the invariants of the reporting metamodel. The script then recomputes the revenue and the units sold from the shop data with AQL and compares them with the report; `test` stops the script if a figure differs. You can recompute the figures by hand from `shop-data.xmi`: the laptops alone account for 4200.0 of the 6815.0.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Code Generation with MTL") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            MTL templates generate artefacts from models. We'll create templates that generate a Swift API, a JSON schema with JSON data, and Markdown documentation from the sales report.

            This demonstrates how MTL produces several kinds of artefacts from a single model, all consistent with each other because they share one source.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the Swift generation template.

                @Code(name: "GenerateSwiftClasses.mtl", file: workflow-01-step-13-swift-templates.mtl)

                The template generates `Codable` structures for the report and its metrics, functions for derived figures such as the top category and the products out of stock, and a `generated` value that holds the figures of the model. `['['/]` writes a literal square bracket, which would otherwise start an MTL expression.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the JSON generation template.

                @Code(name: "GenerateJSONAPI.mtl", file: workflow-01-step-14-json-templates.mtl)

                One template writes two files: a JSON schema that describes sales reports for web clients, and the report itself as JSON data. `separator(',\n')` puts commas only between the array elements.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the documentation template.

                @Code(name: "GenerateDocumentation.mtl", file: workflow-01-step-15-docs-templates.mtl)

                The documentation lists the key figures and a Markdown table for categories, customers and products. An AQL `select` in the last loop lists the products that are out of stock, so the documentation stays synchronised with the model.
            }

            @Step {
                Generate all artefacts.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-16-generate-code.sh)

                `swift-mtl validate` checks the templates first. Each `swift-mtl generate` then registers the reporting metamodel, reads the report and writes the files of its template into its own output directory.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Integration and Validation") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The final stage integrates the generated artefacts and validates the complete chain. This ensures that the workflow produces working, consistent results, and that it produces them again when the inputs are unchanged.

            Integration checks validate that the generated code compiles, that the generated documentation is complete, and that the figures agree from the source model to every artefact.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Compile the generated Swift code.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-17-compile-swift.sh)

                The script makes a Swift package with the generated API as its library and the generated JSON data as a resource of its test target, then builds the library with `swift build`. The first build takes a little while.
            }

            @Step {
                Test the generated API.

                @Code(name: "SalesReport/Tests/SalesReportTests/TestAPIs.swift", file: workflow-01-step-18-test-apis.swift)

                These Swift Testing tests check the generated figures against the shop data, check that category, customer and product figures add up, find the top performers, cover a report without orders, and decode the generated JSON data to compare them with the generated Swift value. Run them with `swift test --package-path SalesReport`. With only the Command Line Tools installed on macOS, building Swift Testing tests occasionally fails with "plugin for module 'TestingMacros' not found"; running `swift test` again usually succeeds.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the generated documentation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-19-validate-docs.sh)

                Plain shell commands check the generated Markdown: `grep` finds each section, a small function counts the table rows, and `test` compares the counts with what AQL counts in the report model. The key figures must match the model, and no unexpanded template text may remain.
            }

            @Step {
                Perform end-to-end workflow validation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-01-step-20-end-to-end-validation.sh)

                The script runs the whole chain again for the shop data and the two valid test instances: validate, transform, validate the report, generate the artefacts and type-check the Swift code with `swiftc -typecheck`. For each input, the revenue that AQL computes from the source model must appear in the report, the Swift code, the JSON data and the Markdown. Finally, `diff` confirms that the shop data produce exactly the same report and artefacts as before, and `shasum` prints checksums that you can record to detect later changes.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What is the correct order for a complete MDE workflow?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Metamodel design → Instance creation → Model transformation → Code generation → Integration

                @Justification(reaction: "Perfect!") {
                    This is the canonical MDE workflow: start with metamodel design, create instances, transform as needed, generate code, then integrate and validate the results.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Code generation → Metamodel design → Instance creation → Transformation → Integration

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Code generation can't come first: you need a metamodel and instances before you can generate code from models.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Instance creation → Metamodel design → Code generation → Transformation → Integration

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect order!") {
                    You need the metamodel before you can create valid instances. The metamodel defines the structure that instances must follow.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How does `swift-ecore validate --metamodel ECommerce.ecore` check business rules such as positive prices?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It compares the instance with a list of known valid shops

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Validation does not compare instances with each other. It checks each instance against the metamodel it conforms to.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                It evaluates the OCL invariants of the metamodel for every object they apply to

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly right!") {
                    The invariants in the OCL annotations of a class are checked for each of its objects, and every violation is reported with the object and its location.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It only checks that the XML is well-formed

                @Justification(reaction: "It does much more!") {
                    Besides reading the document, validation checks classes, feature types, multiplicities and references against the metamodel, and evaluates its invariants.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What role does model transformation play in the MDE workflow?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                It adapts models to meet different system requirements

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Transformations reshape models for different purposes: operational to analytical, platform-independent to platform-specific, or domain-specific adaptations.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It's only needed when working with multiple metamodels

                @Justification(reaction: "Not just for multiple metamodels!") {
                    Even single-metamodel projects often benefit from transformations, for example to refine a model or to create specialised views of it.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It replaces the need for code generation

                @Justification(reaction: "They work together!") {
                    Transformation and code generation are complementary. Transformation prepares models, then code generation produces artefacts from the transformed models.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How should you validate the complete MDE workflow?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Test each step in isolation only

                @Justification(reaction: "End-to-end testing is crucial!") {
                    While individual step testing is important, end-to-end validation ensures the entire workflow produces correct and consistent results.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Combine individual step validation with end-to-end workflow testing

                @Justification(reaction: "Perfect approach!") {
                    Each step is validated on its own, and an end-to-end run checks that figures computed from the source model appear unchanged in every artefact and that the same inputs reproduce the same outputs.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Only validate the final generated code

                @Justification(reaction: "Too late in the process!") {
                    Validation throughout the workflow catches issues early and ensures quality at every step, not just at the end.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
